{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1488410-scale-for-social-influence","id":1488410,"title":"Scale for social influence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-07-29T16:54:34-07:00","id":1488410,"title":"Scale for social influence","content":"So, the Hypnotic Charm flair (Origin p. 152) gives the antagonist this ability: &quot;The effective Scale of whatever she is asking for is considered to be two lower than it actually is.&quot;<br /> <br /> Similarly, the Quill of Ma'at (Hero p. 216) has a knack that &quot;reduces the Scope of any attempt to influence that character&quot; when you write someone's true name. (I assume &quot;Scope&quot; is a typo of &quot;Scale,&quot; since &quot;Scope&quot; doesn't appear to be a rules term.)<br /> <br /> The intrigue rules don't really talk about the Scale of <i>requests</i>, though (and neither do the Scale charts). The Encourage Behavior section just says that the target gets +1 to +3 Enhancement depending on the &quot;apparent danger or cost&quot; of the request (and suggests making it a complex action if it would be higher than that).<br /> <br /> Is there a guidelines anywhere for how to judge the Scale of a request? Of course, every character is different, but it wouldn't hurt to have some general examples. I suppose since a Scale difference of more than three generally results in automatic success or failure, we might infer that a Scale 4+ request is something that a person would never, ever do without magical persuasion?<br /> <br /> Has anyone actually applied the Scale rules in this context before? I'm tempted to work up some examples, but I don't want to reinvent the wheel if someone smarter than me already did this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11237-jefepato","name":"Jefepa